ows how to  process arrow memory that that algorithm  is automatically reusable so you know  it's very difficult right now if I write  an algorithm that processes a panda's  data frame I can't take that code and  process an our data frame because the  memory is different  using the same technology we've been  developing tools to enable data to be  shared between processes without copying  so this is about making it really cheap  and easy to access very large data sets  and to blur the boundary between data  and memory and data on disk so if you  have a really huge data set that is on  your laptop on a solid-state drive maybe  it's a terabyte of data maybe it's 100  gigabytes of data maybe there's only a  small chunk of that data that you want  to use to for your analysis with a lot  of data serialization tools in order to  access that subset of data for your  analysis you have to read the whole data  set so if it might be like the middle of  a CSV file so you have to parse the  whole CSV file before you can get that  middle chunk so arrow makes that that  problem go away where you can just you  know access only the data that you need  and you can get access to that very  cheaply we've been using we've been  using arrow to build file formats so you  may have heard of this thing called  feather that that Hadley and I made so  that was a first example of building a  file format that that uses arrow and I  think that I think that we'll see more  of that that sort of thing you know  develop and in the next couple of years  and so I know in the our world there's a  there's another competing file format  with feather called  called FST and so the way I would  contrast or way I would describe FST is  as an evolved version of feather that  can only be read and R and so from my  point of view like having file formats  that can be read in any programming  languages is a you know kind of that's  that's really what I would what I would  like to see one of the biggest the the  project has grown so much is it's no  longer just about data frames and  columnar data but the columnar data  format is one of the biggest parts of  the project where we spent the most time  designing a suitably general and  efficient data frame representation for  for analytics so I won't go through  these details but these are things you  can read more about we've been building  libraries in many programming languages  so we started out with Java and C++ we  now have a native JavaScript  implementation it's really cool it can  you can generate if an aero file and in  Java or Python and send it to the  browser and read it without doing any  parsing so it doesn't go through JSON or  any of that and and that's really neat  we just received a donation of a go  implementation which was created by  influx data which which builds in flux  DV I need to update this diagram because  we  have a burgeoning rust native rust  implementation which is really cool I  know there's also a Julia implementation  somewhere in the ether that I hope can  get merged into the end of the Apache  project and we've been building bindings  so I've been working on with you know  many other people on Python bindings  there's also Ruby bindings which are  kind of it's like kind of a conception  where it's like bindings on bindings so  Ruby is going through C which is talking  to C++ so you know it's you know you  have bindings to bindings to bindings  starting to see Aero used in some pretty  cool applications so if you follow work  happening in the Berkeley Ryze lab which  is oriented around real time secure  machine learning they've developed a  machine learning framework called ray  which uses they developed a shared  memory object store for managing data  that flows between tasks in a in a  reinforcement learning setting and so  that object store is called plasma and  they donated that to the to the Aero  project and it's become a tightly  integrated component so that we can see  more machine learning systems built on  top of the mantra of free data access  and shared and shared memory which these  libraries make make so you know much  simpler than doing it doing it by hand  many companies in industry have been  have been contributing to the  contributing to the project so I won't  go through all of them so it's really  great to see you know some bigger  companies you know like folks from  Brooks from Intel and HP and you know so  I've been I've been contributing through  through to Sigma and of course I was a  cloud era before that but this is you  know we're getting a lot of inbound  interest and I think you know over the  course of this year and next year I  think we'll see a major uptick in kind  of an investment from from from the big  tech companies who they want to support  Aero in their and their systems we're up  to 140 contributors on github this is  our kind of JIRA burndown chart it turns  out that not everyone uses JIRA but you  can see that we kind of got off to a  slow start and then there was an  election point at the end of last middle  of last year and so it's been kind of  you know kind of to the moon since then  I think that trend will will continue  over over the next over that couple of  years we've made nine major releases so  I'm not going to bore you with the  details of what these releases but just  want to give you a flavor of what's  coming in the project and how it's  relevant to today's science users so  let's talk about computational libraries  because at the end of the day what this  is about for data science users is  making data access a great deal faster  and more efficient making it easier to  use multiple programming languages so if  you want to use Python and art together  this technology makes that much Neos  unit you know much simpler and so I  don't know if you saw the reticulate  package from our studio which provides  python integration and R so having you  know kind of arrow support to be able to  move data between Python and R will make  that whole stack a great deal more  powerful so at a high level you know the  way I think about the problem having  worked in this ecosystem for a long time  is that Python and are built on this  computational foundation that was  established in the mid-1990s we're  looking backward from say 1995 everyone  was programming in Fortran and it was  you know you said hey wouldn't it be  great if we had interpreted programming  languages that could manipulate the same  code the same linear algebra libraries  multi-dimensional arrays that we're  using in Fortran but we could do so in  an interactive interpreted environment  so hence we had numpy developed in  Python and equivalent types of tools in  R but there's a lot of problems with the  the computational systems that perform  analytics in memory so a lot of the  algorithms are single threaded there  isn't a great deal of you know the  execution model the way that algorithms  are evaluated is is very much you know  one step at a time there's a lot of  temporary data that's produced so so  there's a bunch of problems there and  what's happening is that you look in the  Python Antartica system and people are  you know if your data is small  everything's fine and so it turns out  that a lot of people have small data and  this is just not an issue but as soon as  you start getting into you  the tens or hundreds of gigabytes of  data you start to get into into  struggles Ville and so and I've been  there and really you think about where  things are going we've got these  machines with tons of RAM tons of like  very fast solid-state drives and loss of  cores we should be able to work with 100  gigabyte data sets on a laptop without  even thinking twice about it and so  that's that's the world that I'd like to  I'd like to get to so to have Oliver as  many of our algorithms be multi-core GPU  accelerated we have all this fancy code  generation stuff that we can employ to  make things even even faster there's a  lot of there's a lot of sophistication  that we can bring to the table and a lot  of this comes directly out of the  analytic database world so it's not like  we even have to reinvent wheels we just  look at what the database world is doing  and we adapt those ideas for for data  science use cases so there's a wealth of  literature and learning that we can that  we can bring to to the everyday lives of  of data scientists so so what a lot of  the work I'm running out of time but a  lot of work the next couple of years  we'll be building computational  libraries that that process the arrow  memory format so these will be native  code libraries that are that create  shared libraries C C++ shared libraries  so we can use them in Python we can use  them in R we can use them in Java  through j'ni bindings you know I think  we'll see computational libraries build  and rust and other languages and so  those could also be brought brought to  the table so all this will be portable  code that can be used in any and any  programming language it's plenty of  other stuff we got to do you know we got  to read CSV files everyone loves to read  CSV files but it it has to happen  because it's really important but you  know my goal in the long in the long run  and where I hope you know we we see  ourselves in a decade is that we have  you know portable high quality high  performance libraries that we can that  we can develop the front end that suits  us whether that's an our front end or a  Python front end and we have the you  know the same quality of you know  experience and performance and  scalability regardless of what  programming language we're using so  that's that's all I'm sorry for  for running over time but you know with  so with Ursa Labs I will have a lot more  time and more leverage to work on these  problems so that's super exciting I have  about like a 15 page  tech vision document that you can read  all about this stuff  so I've I've been thinking about